00:00:04.370 --> 00:00:08.390 align:start position:0%
for four weeks America engulfed in  protests every day all over the US even  as the country faces the continued  threat of a deadly pandemic sparked by  his death they chanted his name and it  morphed into a movement calling for  police reforms sometimes it was violent  but mostly it was peaceful we asked  people to describe what America is now  feeling what is the mood in America  right now what would you say it is I  think people are tired I think people  are fed up I think people want change  and I think people are willing to do  whatever it takes to get there it's a  lot of hurt it's a lot of pain it's  people who have just felt that for so  long we've been pleading about police  brutality systemic racism it's  heartbreaking but I think that there's  also a tremendous sense of inspiration  and hope and empowerment by the fact  that everyone is coming together in this  beautiful unified way on Friday millions  of people in America are expected to  celebrate what's called Juneteenth an  unofficial holiday commemorating the end  of slavery in the United States this  year in the wake of the nationwide  protests there is a growing number of  calls to make it an official federal  holiday just here in New York on Friday  there are more than 20 different  protests that are planned for Juneteenth  as people see it as a great opportunity  to take to the streets to continue to  let their message be heard in a country  where moods have encompassed anger to  fear to hope in an exhausting yet  unforgettable four weeks in American  history  Gabe Rose Ando al Jazeera New York
 

